---
import PolicyLayout from '~/layouts/PolicyLayout.astro';
---

<PolicyLayout
  title="How In-Browser File Conversion Works"
  description="How Formatoza converts files without uploading them: browser decoders, WebAssembly, Web Workers and lazy loading — and how you can verify nothing is uploaded."
  path="/how-it-works/"
  h1="How in-browser conversion works"
  lead="Formatoza sends the converter to your file, instead of sending your file to a converter. Here is what that means in practice."
>
  <h2>The usual way: upload, process, download</h2>
  <p>
    Most online converters are thin web pages in front of a server. You upload your file, it waits
    in a queue, a server converts it, and you download the result. That requires trusting the
    service with your file, and it's slow for large files on ordinary connections.
  </p>
  <h2>The Formatoza way: your browser does the work</h2>
  <p>
    Every Formatoza page is a static web page. When you open a converter, your browser downloads the
    code for that converter and runs it locally:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Images</strong> are decoded with your browser's own image decoders and re-encoded with
      the canvas API. Formats browsers don't support — HEIC, TIFF, ICO bitmaps — are decoded by
      small libraries or WebAssembly modules that run in the page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PDFs</strong> are rendered with PDF.js, Mozilla's PDF engine, and created with
      pdf-lib.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data files</strong> (CSV, JSON, XML, YAML, XLSX) are parsed and written in a
      background Web Worker, so large files don't freeze the page — and cancelling stops the work
      instantly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Markup, encodings and subtitles</strong> are transformed with purpose-built
      JavaScript.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Word documents</strong> are read with Mammoth, and the resulting HTML is sanitised
      before you see it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Heavy components load only when needed. The homepage ships almost no JavaScript; the PDF
    renderer loads only on PDF tools once you add a PDF; the HEIC decoder loads only if your browser
    can't decode HEIC itself.
  </p>
  <h2>Verify it yourself</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open a converter and let the page load.</li>
    <li>
      Open your browser's developer tools and switch to the <em>Network</em> tab.
    </li>
    <li>
      Convert a file. You'll see the converter code load (once), but no request containing your
      file.
    </li>
    <li>
      For extra certainty, disconnect from the internet after the first conversion — the tool keeps
      working offline for the rest of the session.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Limits of local processing</h2>
  <p>
    Your device does the work, so very large files are limited by its memory, and older phones may
    be slower than a server. Each tool states its file-size limits. Some conversions — PDF to Word
    with layout fidelity, or large video files — aren't offered because they can't be done reliably
    in a browser yet. We would rather not offer a tool than offer one that produces poor results.
  </p>
  <h2>What still goes over the network</h2>
  <p>
    Loading the website itself (pages, code, fonts) and, when active, advertising. Ads are loaded
    after the page has finished loading and never receive your files or their contents. See the{' '}
    <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for details.
  </p>
</PolicyLayout>
